[[mapping-material-sample-information]]
== Mapping material sample information

[IMPORTANT]
====
Information about individual material samples should be saved to the `occurrence` table. Links between a material sample and the organism or sample it was taken from should be saved to the `resourceRelationship` table.
====

Many surveys collect physical material as well as recording observations. A <<materialSample, *material sample*>> may be a whole organism kept as a specimen, part of an organism (e.g., a tissue or blood sample), a group of organisms processed together, an environmental sample (e.g., soil or water), or material derived from any of these (e.g., a DNA extract). Sharing information about material samples lets data users know that physical material exists, find where it is kept, and connect laboratory results (such as pathogen tests or genetic analyses) back to the survey Event and organism they came from.

Material sample information is captured at three levels in a Darwin Core Archive for survey data:

* *At the Event level*, in the `survey` table: whether material samples or vouchers were collected during each Event, and what kinds. +
* *At the Occurrence level*, in the `occurrence` table: the identity, preparation, and storage of each specimen or sample. +
* *Between records*, in the `resourceRelationship` and `emof` tables: how samples relate to one another and any measurements or facts about them.

[NOTE]
.*Material samples in GBIF Event datasets*
====
In the current GBIF data model, an Event core dataset does not have a separate table for material samples. Each specimen or sample is shared as a record in the `occurrence` table, using term:dwc[dwc:basisOfRecord] to indicate the nature of the record and Occurrence extension terms to describe the material. GBIF also lists a set of https://rs.gbif.org/extensions.html[GGBN material sample extensions^]; these are not designed for use with an Event core dataset.
====

=== Event level: declaring material samples and vouchers

For each Event, indicate whether material samples were collected using http://rs.tdwg.org/eco/terms/hasMaterialSamples[`eco:hasMaterialSamples`^] and list the types collected in http://rs.tdwg.org/eco/terms/materialSampleTypes[`eco:materialSampleTypes`^]. If any material was accessioned into a collection as a <<voucher, voucher>>, also populate http://rs.tdwg.org/eco/terms/hasVouchers[`eco:hasVouchers`^] and http://rs.tdwg.org/eco/terms/voucherInstitutions[`eco:voucherInstitutions`^]. See link:#material-samples[Material samples] and link:#vouchers[Vouchers] for details.

Every voucher is a material sample, but not every material sample is a voucher. A material sample becomes a voucher when it is accessioned into a museum or other collection in support of a specific project or survey.

=== Occurrence level: describing each material sample

Each specimen or sample is a row in the `occurrence` table, linked to the Event during which it was collected through term:dwc[dwc:eventID] and identified by a unique term:dwc[dwc:occurrenceID]. In addition to the required Occurrence terms (see link:#mapping-occurrence-information[Mapping Occurrence information]):

* *Basis of record:* Use term:dwc[dwc:basisOfRecord] to state what kind of material the record represents. Use `PreservedSpecimen` for an organism (or part of an organism) that has been preserved, `LivingSpecimen` for a living organism kept in a collection, and `MaterialSample` for a sample taken for further study (e.g., a tissue sample, a pooled sample, or a DNA extract). +
* *Sample identifier:* Report the identifier assigned to the sample in term:dwc[dwc:materialSampleID]. Use the same identifier that appears on the sample label or in laboratory records so that the record can be traced back to the physical sample. +
* *Collection identifiers:* If the sample is housed in a collection, report its catalog number in term:dwc[dwc:catalogNumber] and identify the holding institution and collection using term:dwc[dwc:institutionCode] (or term:dwc[dwc:institutionID]) and term:dwc[dwc:collectionCode]. Any other numbers assigned to the sample can be listed in term:dwc[dwc:otherCatalogNumbers]. +
* *Preparation and storage:* Describe how the material was prepared or preserved in term:dwc[dwc:preparations] (e.g., `95% ethanol`) and its current status in term:dwc[dwc:disposition] (e.g., `in collection`, `missing`). +
* *Remarks:* Any further information about the material sample that does not fit another term can be shared in term:dwc[dwc:materialEntityRemarks]. +
* *Genetic sequences:* If sequences were generated from the sample and deposited in a public repository (e.g., GenBank), list their identifiers in term:dwc[dwc:associatedSequences]. The DNA-derived data extension cannot currently be published with an Event core dataset; see link:#dna-and-metabarcoding-data[DNA and metabarcoding data] for guidance on publishing DNA-derived data.

